WEBVTT

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:07.090
Oh no, I think I included too much information

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.250 
in my presentation

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.100
I won’t be able to finish it in the time I have

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.240
How much time do you have for your presentation? 

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:20.200
The average length is usually about twenty minutes

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.140
I only have fifteen minutes

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:26.200
And I have a lot more to say than that

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.100
You need to do a good job managing the time

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.200
Look at your structure outlines

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.200
plan a schedule accordingly

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:37.210 
and allocate time into each section

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.100
OK. Now I see what my problem is

00:00:42.1200 --> 00:00:44.200 
I wrote too much for the introduction

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.300 
How do I adjust it?

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.270
I suggest you rehearse your talk first

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.220 
and find out how much talking time

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:55.150 
you are using for each section

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.170
Isn’t that called a “timed rehearsal”? 

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:02.060
Do I need a timekeeper or a stopwatch 

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.150
to keep track of time?

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.500
Yes. Time each section carefully

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.280
If you’re talking time is longer than the allotted time

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.000
adjust your script.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:15.100
Right. You need to be flexible

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.200 
For example, if you really need more time

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:22.110 
for an explanation and examples in the body

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.110
make the opening and ending shorter

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.220
If the presentation becomes too long

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.300 
the audience will lose interest by the end

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.070
I know I need to keep the whole presentation 

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:36.300
within the given time

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:39.240
Another tip for good time management 

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:41.300
is to always start on time

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.290
I know a lot of presentations go over

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:48.200
their allotted time just because they start late

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.230
Right, and remember, even with the best preparation

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:56.300 
something unexpected might occur during the presentation

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.300
And that will extend the overall program

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:05.200 
Try to keep to the time and be ready to adapt 

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000 
A written schedule can help you keep track of time easily

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.190 
It’s quite effective and efficient in helping you 

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.300 
to convey your ideas in the allocated time

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:21.100 
Wow, look at the time. I’d better go

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.100 
I enjoyed talking with you.